{
  "info": {
    "name": "Sarfees — Group Trip (3 passengers, one driver)",
    "description": "Tests Stage-1 GROUPING: three separate passengers book the same corridor (Irbid → Amman) at the same departure time, the engine bundles them into ONE trip group, and one driver serves all of them in a single multi-stop trip.\n\n## How the timing works\n- Passenger A books a **scheduled** trip departing at the next quarter-hour slot at least 31 min out (a pre-request script computes this and shares it via `groupTravelDate`) — pickup times must land on :00/:15/:30/:45 and be ≥ 30 min ahead.\n- B and C book the same corridor/time within seconds — the group is still OPEN (freeze only happens at T-30), so they join A's group.\n- When the departure crosses T-30 (between ~1 and ~15 min after booking, depending on where the quarter-hour falls) the sweeper freezes the group and cascades the offer to the top-ranked driver.\n\n## Why the pickup points are close together\nThe compatibility engine enforces a wait tolerance (default 12 min): adding a member is rejected if the extra pickup legs would delay an existing member past their tolerance (`wait_tolerance_exceeded`). The three pickups here are ~1 km apart so every insertion passes. Spread them across town and passenger C gets their own group instead — try it to see the rejection in the API logs.\n\n## ⚠️ If the driver's REAL mobile app is running\nThe driver app pings GPS every ~10 s, and every ping overwrites the matcher's location snapshot — it beats whatever `currentLocationLat/Lng` this collection sends in activate. The cascade hard-filters drivers to the ORIGIN city, so **the trip's departure city must be the city where the driver's phone physically is**, or the driver is silently excluded and the offer goes to other drivers (then escalates). This collection books **Irbid → Amman** with pickups in north Irbid; if you're testing from Amman (or with no real app open), flip `departureCityId`/`arrivalCityId` and swap the departure/arrival coordinates.\n\n## Run order\n1. **Auth** — OTP logins for passengers A/B/C and the driver (mock OTP `1234`).\n2. **Driver online** — activate with GPS in the pickup area.\n3. **Passengers book** — A creates the group, B and C join it. Run 3.1 → 3.2 → 3.3 back-to-back — the group stays OPEN (joinable) until T-30.\n4. **Matching** — re-run 4.1 every ~30 s until `pendingOffer` appears (up to ~15 min — the freeze fires when the quarter-hour departure crosses T-30). Offer has a 30 s countdown — accept promptly.\n5. **Assign** — accept returns the manifest. Test asserts `summary.passengerCount === 3` (grouping proof) and saves the ordered stop list.\n6. **Execute** — one explicit request pair per stop: `Stop N — arrive` then `Stop N — confirm`. The accept step (5.1) saves each stop's id, endpoint (confirm-pickup vs confirm-dropoff) and body into `stopN*` variables, so every request just runs in order — no re-running. Slots for stops that don't exist on this trip skip themselves automatically (up to 6 stops are pre-wired: 3 pickups + 3 dropoffs worst case).\n\n## Prereqs\n- Seeded passengers `790000001/2/3` + driver `770000001` (Contabo test data).\n- Driver has no leftover active trip.\n- Responses are wrapped `{ code, message, data }`.",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
